\subsubsection{Migration from Vue2 to Vue3 and adoption of new trame best
practices in the tutorial}

Vue3 (and Vuetify3 \cite{Vuetify}) is now the standard frontend framework
supported by trame. The trame API has also evolved, introducing a new
recommended development style. As a result, some syntax changes were required,
while others were strongly encouraged to align with current best practices.

The trame tutorial was the first component to be updated. This required
modifying all examples in the
\href{https://github.com/Kitware/trame-tutorial}{trame-tutorial} repository, as
well as the corresponding \citev{VitePress} documentation in the
\href{https://github.com/Kitware/trame}{trame} repository.

\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-tutorial/7}, \cref{pr:Kitware/trame-tutorial/8},
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-tutorial/9}, and \cref{pr:Kitware/trame/877} contain the
pull requests (PRs) implementing these changes.

\subsubsection{Making all examples functional}

The trame examples showcase the flexibility of trame across a wide range of use
cases and libraries. They also demonstrate the usage of many trame widgets and
often serve as practical starting points for users. For instance, a developer
wishing to integrate Markdown into a trame application can use the
trame-markdown example as a template.

\cref{pr:Kitware/trame/876} restores functionality across all examples. Most
issues originated from API changes in third-party dependencies. A requirements
file with pinned versions is intentionally not provided, as the goal is for
examples to remain compatible with the latest versions and features of external
libraries while demonstrating their interoperability with trame.

Because trame documentation is not yet fully comprehensive, many features can
currently only be learned by studying example code. The examples therefore
complement the tutorial and were updated to follow the same best practices
introduced in the previous maintenance effort.

\cref{pr:Kitware/trame/879} is a follow-up fix addressing a typo introduced by
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame/876} that broke documentation generation. Additionally,
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame/882} and \cref{pr:Kitware/trame/883} fix the last
remaining example involving \citev{Docker} and \citev{ParaView}. An issue was
opened and subsequently closed to track this work:
\href{https://github.com/Kitware/trame/issues/875}{trame/issues/875}.

\subsubsection{Fixes to trame-cookiecutter \cref{repo:trame-cookiecutter}}

\citev{Cookiecutter} is a file structure template format and engine. With a
cookiecutter template it is possible to quickly create a project file structure
only from few parameters.

The trame cookiecutter template had received limited testing and did not
function correctly in all situations. The fixes are implemented in
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-cookiecutter/26},
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-cookiecutter/27},
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-cookiecutter/28}, and
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-cookiecutter/30}.

\subsubsection{Update pre-commit hooks and make the linter pass in trame-vtk
\cref{repo:trame-vtk}}

\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-vtk/108} updates the trame-vtk pre-commit configuration
\cite{precommit} to match the one used in trame-cookiecutter
\cref{repo:trame-cookiecutter}.

\citev{Ruff} is a Python linter that detects stylistic issues and common bad
practices. Maintaining consistent style and coding standards is particularly
important in collaborative software development because it makes code easier to
understand, review, and maintain. The linting rules introduced by the new
pre-commit configuration are relatively strict, requiring numerous style and
code-quality fixes throughout the repository.

These rules originate from several linting tools. In this repository, they may
come from \citev{pylint} (rule codes beginning with PLC), \citev{pycodestyle}
(rule codes beginning with E or W), or \citev{flake8} (rule codes beginning with
F or SIM). Examples include:

\begin{itemize}

\item unused-import (F401): Checks for unused imports.

\item print (T201): Checks for print statements.

\item multiple-with-statements (SIM117): Checks for unnecessary nesting of
consecutive context managers.

\item import-outside-top-level (PLC0415): Checks for import statements located
outside a module's top-level scope, such as inside functions or class
definitions.

\end{itemize}
